course: dictionary
lesson: 1674
title: Choosy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Choosy is an informal adjective. It describes someone who takes great care over what they pick and is hard to please, especially about food, clothes or friends. «Choosy pets nearly always have a reason.» The clue is in the word itself: choose. A choosy pet picks carefully, so it is not happy with just anything.
explain: Choosy can be a fair word or a slightly critical one. It depends on the situation. A choosy shopper who checks every label is being careful. A choosy friend who will only sit with certain people may sound a little unkind. Read the words around it to decide which feeling the writer means.
explain: Look at the saying «Beggars can't be choosers». It means that if you really need help, you must accept what you are given, even if it is not what you wanted. Choosers are people who get to choose, and the saying tells you that being choosy is not always possible.
fact: Choosy means very careful about what you choose, and hard to please.
fact: Choosy is an informal word, so you will hear it in everyday talk more than in formal writing.
fact: Choosy is often used about food, clothes and friends.
fact: «Choosy pets nearly always have a reason.» Here choosy shows that the pets pick carefully.
fact: In nature lessons, some animals are choosy about their mates or their food.
exercise: What does choosy mean? | Happy to take anything that is offered | *Very careful about what you choose, and hard to please | Quick to make up your mind | Very loud and noisy
exercise: «Beggars can't be choosers» means | Beggars are always very choosy | *If you really need help, you must accept what you are given | Choosers should give money to beggars | Only people with lots of money can choose
exercise: «Choosy pets nearly always have a reason.» What does choosy show about the pets? | They are always asleep | They are always hungry | They are very loud | *They pick carefully, for a good reason
test: Mum says, «You're too choosy, just eat what's on your plate.» What does she mean? | *You are being hard to please about food | You are being very quick at eating | You need a bigger plate | You are being very kind to the cook
test: Choosy is an informal word. Where would you most likely hear it? | In a science textbook as a technical term | *In everyday talk with friends and family | In a formal speech at a royal event | In a formal legal document
exam: Which meaning fits «choosy» best? | *Hard to please and careful about what you pick | Happy to take whatever you get | Tall and thin | Fast and noisy
